## Part B — Relocation: the workbench moves home; test scenes get their own launcher

### B.0 Problem statement

The screen users actually see is `TableAndElementRotationView.mainView` in
**`OpticalConstructor.TestWindows`** (`…TestWindows/TableAndElementRotationView.fs:2367`),
hosted by `MainConstructorWindow` (`…App/Program.fs:118-150`). The
`OpticalConstructor.Ui` Elmish shell is dead code for the Main flow — the
composition comment records that the old constructor shell "is kept but no
longer opened by the launcher's Main button" (`…App/Program.fs:112-117`).
The operator's decision (005/Q26–Q27): the workbench REAL-MOVES into
`OpticalConstructor.Ui` (namespaces follow the project; no file-linking; one
`.fsproj` per folder), the dead shell is retired, and the seven diagnostic
scenes currently on the product launcher (`…App/Program.fs:177-224`) move to
a tiny new executable `OpticalConstructor.TestWindows.App`, after which
`OpticalConstructor.App` drops its `OpticalConstructor.TestWindows` project
reference entirely — test code leaves the product dependency graph.

### B.1 What stays alive through the retirement

`UserEnvironment.fs` is live (`…App/Program.fs:37`) and stays. The Fluent
theme seam the `App` type uses (`AppShell.themeVariant`,
`…App/Program.fs:244`) survives as a function — kept or moved into a retained
module — while the rest of the dead shell (the `MainWindow` host, `Shell.fs`,
`Ribbon.fs`, `MaterialsView.fs`, `ConstructionPage.fs`, `ConstructorView.fs`,
`LifecycleView.fs`, `Templates.fs`, `Help.fs`, and every Ui module plus
Ui.Tests test left without a live consumer by those deletions) is removed,
not paralleled. The product launcher keeps only `Main` until Part N restores
the full four-button row — every intermediate state runs.

