## Part C — appsettings, app-scoped composition, and the WindowLauncher seam

### C.0 Problem statement

The five domain proxies are created INSIDE `MainConstructorWindow`
(`…App/Program.fs:129-147`), so a second window cannot share the stores; and
no `appsettings.json` exists (user preferences live in `environment.json`,
`…Ui/UserEnvironment.fs:181-185`). This part: (1) `appsettings.json` read
ONLY through `Softellect.Sys.AppSettings.AppSettingsProvider`
(`Softellect.Sys` **10.1.301.54**, already referenced by
`…Storage/OpticalConstructor.Storage.fsproj`; its `tryCreate` opens
`appsettings.json`, its `SetOnMissing = true` writes missing keys' defaults
back, and it exposes typed `get*OrDefault` accessors and
`tryGetConnectionString`) with ONE `ConfigKey` constants module and typed
values flowing inward as a pure Domain settings record of elevated types —
initial inventory `SelectWindowsModal = false`, `QuickPickThreshold = 5`,
`TreeAutoBuildThreshold = 100`, `ThicknessBucketCap = 8`, plus the Part M
`connectionStrings` entries; (2) the proxies hoist to ONE app-scope
composition built at startup and injected everywhere; (3) window policy
behind one seam — `<SVC:WindowLauncher>` over a host-layer `WindowRegistry`
(mutable host state OUTSIDE the Elmish model, the registered-storage-provider
pattern at `…App/Program.fs:96` in today's tree): Materials/Library windows
are single-instance (a second open activates; a Select-state open re-targets),
editor windows are multi-instance keyed by the edited entity's id, and NEW
entities mint their Guid at Add-window open carrying
`EntryFreshness = NewUnsaved | Persisted` — moving the id-mint off the save
path (`newMaterialId` at `…TestWindows/MaterialEditorView.fs:416`). The seam
generalizes the existing `EditorLaunchers` function-record
(`…TestWindows/TableAndElementRotationView.fs:116-141`). Select-state windows
open modal (`ShowDialog`, owner = requesting window) when the
`SelectWindowsModal` switch is true, non-modal when false (default false);
Browse windows always non-modal.

