## Part D — The faceted engine (Domain, pure)

### D.0 Problem statement

One faceted-navigation core powers both windows: items + attribute
definitions → a filterable, reconfigurable tree with live counts. Pure F# in
a new `…Domain/Facets.fs` (plus bucketing and the concrete facet catalogues):
`AttributeDef<'item>` with `appliesTo` (dependent facets vanish entirely when
inapplicable) and a LIST-valued `extract` (multi-valued facets first-class —
the dispersion-model facet is per axis/segment over the ten `DispersionModel`
cases, `…Domain/DispersionModels.fs:168-178`, so counts need not sum to the
total); constraints as values (discrete key-set OR within a facet, numeric
range); the engine as naive folds (`filter`, `countFor`, breadcrumb
after-counts, `buildTree`) — corpus sizes are tens to hundreds, no indices.
Physics-derived offers reuse `availableGyrationClasses`
(`…Domain/MaterialComplexityEditor.fs:373`), never re-derived. Numeric
bucketing follows the agreed 1–2–5 log-ladder algorithm (empty buckets
dropped, fewest-first merging under `ThicknessBucketCap`, half-open
`[lo, hi)`, exact-value singleton, nm below 1 µm / µm above, recomputed on
every constraint change, manual min–max as an ordinary constraint chip).
Material facets extract from `MaterialEntry.properties`
(`…Domain/MaterialLibrary.fs:230-238`); sample facets lift material facets as
the distinct union over constituent materials, plus substrate material
(Plate/Wedge only), "has thin films", film materials, and per-film thickness
buckets. Tree behaviour: **search order ≠ representation order** — applied
constraints never reshuffle the tree; a breadcrumb strip shows applied
constraints in application order, each removable with its after-count;
count-preview on every offered value; zero-count branches never shown; result
count always live, tree materialization gated above
`TreeAutoBuildThreshold`.

