## Part G — Select mode, Selector flow, pre-binding, sample-editor picking

### G.0 Problem statement

**Selector = Library in a Select state** — the same window in code, never a
copy: `LibraryWindowMode = Browse | Select of SelectionContext` where the
context carries the pre-applied, non-removable kind constraint, the typed
target (table element | sample-layer position), and `onSelected` /
`onCancelled`. Select mode adds exactly two buttons — Select (returns the
highlighted entry, closes) and Close (cancels) — everything else IS the
ordinary window, so add-on-the-fly works because it is the library.
Staleness (non-modal default): a changed table selection or a vanished target
cancels/closes the open Select window (the pending-bind-clears precedent,
`…TestWindows/TableAndElementRotationView.fs:941-944`); a second Choose
closes the first; `onSelected` dispatches a targeted message — a vanished
target is a no-op plus a status line, never a throw. The Selector bay flow:
Choose… opens the Library window kind-constrained; the inline quick-pick
strip renders ONLY when the kind-constrained entry count is under
`QuickPickThreshold`; both paths converge on the SAME bind message that
commits `placement.valueId`
(`…TestWindows/TableAndElementRotationView.fs:768,780`). Pre-binding
(operator, Q7/Q28): every new table element except a sample is created
pre-bound to its seeded default — light source → `src-600`, detector →
`det-intensity`, and the polarizer palette exposes THREE add buttons LP / CPL
/ CPR pre-binding `pol-lp` / `pol-cp-left` / `pol-cp-right` with
`CatalogueKind` UNCHANGED (`…Domain/Placement.fs:93-101`). Samples stay
unbound (the inverse hook). The silent ideal-analyzer fallback — the
`Option.defaultValue Library.IdealLinear` tail of `runAnalyzerKind`
(`…TestWindows/TableAndElementRotationView.fs:1407-1427`) — is retired in
favour of reading the bound entry, with a typed "no analyzer present" status
when the scene has none. The sample editor's `WrapPanel` material picker
(`…TestWindows/SampleEditorView.fs:740-758`) is replaced by the Materials
window in Select state targeted at a `LayerPosition`; the material list
re-queries the proxy when a Select window returns and on window activation,
replacing the load-once snapshot (`…TestWindows/SampleEditorWindow.fs:32-35`);
live cross-window notifications are out of scope.

---

## Part H — Lifecycle: versioning + supersede, active/inactive

### H.0 Problem statement

Two-level identity (operator, Q18–Q20): `MaterialId` / `SampleId` stay the
stable identities; references (sample layers, experiment descriptors) point
at a **version** (`MaterialVersionId` / `SampleVersionId` over a
`VersionNumber` DU), with `EntryLifecycle = ActiveEntry | InactiveEntry`.
The version-creation rule is ONE pure shared decision: editing a **used**
version and saving a physics change mints the next version — and only if the
physics actually differs (structural equality; an identical save stays put);
an **unused** version mutates in place no matter how often edited; metadata
edits (name, description, category) never version. "Used" = referenced by a
persisted experiment or saved project (operator, Q19); nothing is persisted
yet, so the check is a seam — `versionsInUse : unit -> Set<VersionRef>`
computed from the in-memory experiment store — fully unit-testable now,
truthful later. The library always edits the latest version; older versions
are view-only. Inactive hides an entry from new-use offers (pickers, Select
mode, default facet counts — with a show-inactive/superseded toggle);
resolution of existing references ignores it; superseded versions behave as
inactive automatically. `ProtectedBuiltIn` entries can be neither inactivated
nor superseded. Deletion remains hard-blocked while referenced (the
`MaterialStillReferenced` block, `…Domain/ElementId.fs:755-765`) and
additionally for any used version. Both registered proxies re-shape in place
per §0.2. Export-embeds-versions is a recorded FUTURE decision — no export
work this cycle (§0.3).

---

## Part I — Experiments hold the full setup; scene/experiment IO through proxies

### I.0 Problem statement

`Experiments.Experiment` holds only the varied element today
(`…Domain/ElementId.fs:909-917`). It grows a **setup**: an ordered
element-descriptor list (kind, placement/orientation summary, bound entry
reference by version where versioned) with the varied element identified
within the list; sample and light source are OPTIONAL — `E1 = LS+LP+S+LP(rotate)`,
`E2 = LS+LP+LP(rotate)` (no sample), `E3 =` nothing (dark line) are all
first-class; the detector kind is part of the chain and determines the
expected data-file shape. The name collision goes away: the OLD toggle-based
`Groups.ExperimentCollection` (`…Domain/Groups.fs:114-129`, persisted inside
the groups JSON whose load/save lives in `…Storage/GroupsLibrary.fs` per the
doc at `…Domain/Groups.fs:141-143`) is renamed `WorkbenchToggleSet` (type
rename only — the JSON shape is unchanged), so *experiment collection* means
the live constructor concept exclusively (its `commit`,
`…Domain/ElementId.fs:1010`). Scene and experiment-collection save/load are
proxy seams (`<STORE:SceneProxy>`, `<STORE:ExperimentCollectionProxy>`) with
`createInMemory` implementations and round-trip unit tests — NO disk format,
`.ocproj` stays unwired (operator, Q25); the proxies are the seam a future
storage cycle swaps.

